# From v1.0.0 on, the export libraries (jszip, FileSaver, jsPDF, jspdf-autotable) are
# pasted into the build instead of being fetched from cdnjs, so these files are
# byte-sensitive in a way the older ones were not. core.autocrlf is on for this repo,
# which would rewrite the newlines inside that minified code on checkout — a stray \r
# inside a JS string literal is a data change, not a formatting one, and it would end
# up in generated PDFs.
#
# -text disables newline translation entirely, so the bytes that are committed, the
# bytes Netlify serves, and the bytes that were verified in a browser are the same.
# It also keeps `node source/build.mjs` producing identical output on any machine,
# which is what makes source/verify.mjs meaningful.
#
# Older builds (v0.9.x) are deliberately not listed: they load their libraries from
# the network, so they are ordinary text, and changing their attributes now would
# rewrite files that are already published.

# Built, shipping files.
TheatreCuePlayer_v1.*.html -text

# The workshop: the app source and the pinned library copies it is built from.
source/** -text

# The vendored FFmpeg engine, for exactly the same reason. Git would very likely detect the
# .wasm as binary on its own, but "very likely" is a poor basis for a 32 MB file that either
# runs or does not, and ffmpeg-core.js beside it is emscripten output where a stray \r is a
# data change. Left to autocrlf, nothing would look wrong until the tools page stopped working.
vendor/** -text
